Sequence

0.00 - 1.40
Frame opens already breathing: dense white vapor rolling across the lower pad, orange gantry light burning through latticework, cold black sky above. The rocket stands motionless but alive. The lower structure is mostly hidden, except for a fragmented glimpse of AR through steam and shadow.

1.40 - 2.80
Low-angle base shot through drifting vapor. The letters become clearer for a moment: ARTEX, then disappear again behind a collapsing wave of cryogenic fog. Floodlights reflect in wet black ground like candlelight on stone. The machine feels installed, not parked.

2.80 - 4.20
Mid-body view of the rocket: weathered orange tank, frost seams, practical surface scarring, no decorative futurism. A deep internal hum begins to register in the image through slight vibration and vent response. Small white vapor jets pulse in controlled bursts.

4.20 - 5.60
Tower interior shot through steel lattice. Warm light climbs level by level inside the gantry, not flashy, just sequential and deliberate, like a doctrine tree executing upward. No chaos. No alarm. Only ordered awakening.

5.60 - 7.00
Capsule and upper stage framed against the black sky. Thin vapor strips peel off the hull. Antennas and sensors wake subtly: tiny status lights, a brief glint on an optic surface, minute motion from service arms retracting. The vehicle looks sacred because it is fully functional.

7.00 - 8.40
Return to the lower structure. ARTEX is now visible more fully at the pad base, but still broken by steam columns and glare. The composition makes the name feel discovered, not advertised. The pad behaves like a monument with plumbing.

8.40 - 9.80
Umbilical region, pipes, frost, clamp housings, metal under tension. Vapor density increases. A new tone enters the machinery: deeper, heavier, committed. The camera picks up fine physical trembling through the support structure.

9.80 - 11.00
Wide vertical shot of full rocket and gantry. Interior tower lights are now fully active. Side vents discharge in staggered sequence. The entire pad feels as if it has come online as a single organism. The rocket is still motionless, but the rite is clearly underway.

11.00 - 12.20
Engine-bell region under massive steam wash. A brief ignition pre-flash blooms under the vehicle and vanishes into vapor. Ground reflections jump brighter. The frame shudders from real pressure response, not stylized shake.

12.20 - 13.40
Full ignition. Dense orange-white flame and violent steam eruption pour downward and outward. The lower frame shakes from the shockwave. Gantry steel flickers between light and shadow through exhaust turbulence. The ritual becomes force.

13.40 - 14.80
Hold on the base through chaos: flame, vapor, reflection, structure. ARTEX at the pad base is intermittently legible between waves of steam, as if the name itself is being forged into visibility by ignition.

14.80 - 16.20
Clamp release and first upward motion. Very slow at first, massive, undeniable. The rocket does not leap; it ascends like a command finally granted. The camera struggles slightly to maintain composition under vibration and heat shimmer.

16.20 - 17.60
Long-lens vertical tracking shot as the vehicle climbs past the gantry. Warm tower light and exhaust bloom create alternating bands of illumination across the weathered hull. It feels less like departure than activation of a larger system.

17.60 - 19.00
Low perimeter angle. The rocket rises through collapsing steam banks, turning the launchpad into a roaring industrial sanctuary. Wet ground burns with reflected orange and white. The pad remains monumental even as it is overwhelmed.

19.00 - 20.40
Mid-distance shot. The vehicle is clear of the tower now, ascending with slow sovereign force. No cheering, no triumphant flourish, only machinery carrying out doctrine. The absence of humans makes it feel older, stricter, more absolute.

20.40 - 22.00
Steeper upward track into darkness. Exhaust turbulence distorts the lower frame. The rocket becomes a bright ascending pillar above the glowing skeletal gantry. Below, the ARTEX pad remains lit like a mechanized altar.

22.00 - 23.50
The rocket climbs higher, reducing to a luminous spear. The tower and pad sink into steam and glare. The launch no longer looks like an event. It looks like a system confirming itself.

23.50 - 25.00
Final upward view: the vehicle continues into the black sky, clean and committed. No stage separation, no resolution, no celebration. Cut while ascent is still in progress, leaving the feeling that the doctrine has only just begun.

